JOI:
It used for input validator.When we send the req, if the req is wrong before sending this to db, need some validation, we use this.
Create the input validator.js file. and write the user schema how it should look like, we are creating the userschema with joi object that take 2 fields ie the req body fields adn how that req. here we mentioned min length, required, string then email type. Once schema is created, creating middleware validateInputText, take req, res and have next, because its middleware. We are validating the userschema with its req body ie const {error} = userSchema.validate(req.body); if any error occured we are sending the status 400. On message message: error.details[0].message on error we are gettingdetails first argument. Then we finally call next() to go to next step.

import JOI from "joi";

const userSchema = JOI.object({
    name: JOI.string().min(3).required(),
    email: JOI.string().email().required(),
});

const validateInputText = (req, res, next) => {
    const {error} = userSchema.validate(req.body);
    if(error) {
         return res.status(400).json({
            status: 400,
            message: error.details[0].message
        });
    }
    next();
};

export default validateInputText;

Now we define this but need to call in user routes ie when put and post happend it validate the req body ie router.post("/user",validateInputText, createUser); , here we have called as middleware, so when sending req, if the middleware satisfied then only go to next actual fn ie performing to db.

import express from "express";

import { createUser,
    getAllUser,
    updateUser,
    getUser,
    deleteUser
 } from "../controller/userController.js";
import validateInputText from "../middleware/inputValidator.js";

const router = express.Router();

router.post("/user",validateInputText, createUser);
router.get("/user", getAllUser);
router.put("/user/:id", validateInputText, updateUser);
router.get("/user/:id", getUser);
router.delete("/user/:id", deleteUser);

export default router;